WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.889 --> 00:00:06.650
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:06.650 --> 00:00:11.900
In an earlier episode, we covered a paper
by the name Everybody Dance Now.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:17.660
In this stunning work, we could take a video
of a professional dancer, then record a video

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:23.900
of our own, let’s be diplomatic - less beautiful
moves, and then, transfer the dancer's performance

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.320
onto our own body in the video.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.839
We called this process motion transfer.

00:00:28.839 --> 00:00:34.810
Now, look at this new, also learning-based
technique, that does something similar…where

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:40.300
in goes a description of a pose, just one
image of the target person, and on the other

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.200
side, out comes a proper animation of this
character according to our prescribed motions.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.210
Now, before you think that it means that we
would need to draw and animate stick figures

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:54.219
to use this, I will stress that this is not
the case.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:59.590
There are many techniques that perform pose
estimation, where we just insert a photo,

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:05.170
or even a video, and it creates all these
stick figures for us that represent the pose

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.960
that people are taking in these videos.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.780
This means that we can even have a video of
someone dancing, and just one image of the

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.150
target person, and the rest is history.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.450
Insanity.

00:01:17.450 --> 00:01:22.640
That is already amazing and very convenient,
but this paper works with a video to video

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.370
problem formulation, which is a concept that
is more general than just generating movement.

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:29.759
Way more.

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:35.130
For instance, we can also specify the input
video of us, then add one, or at most a few

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:42.090
images of the target subject, and we can make
them speak and behave using our gestures.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:47.670
This is already absolutely amazing, however,
the more creative minds out there are already

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:53.930
thinking that if we are thinking about images,
it can be a painting as well, right?

00:01:53.930 --> 00:02:02.259
Yes, indeed, we can make the Mona Lisa speak
with it as well.

00:02:02.259 --> 00:02:07.450
It can also take a labeled image, this is
what you see here, where the colored and animated

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:13.590
patches show the object boundaries for different
object classes, then, we take an input photo

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:19.680
of a street scene, and we get photorealistic
footage with all the cars, buildings, and

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:20.680
vegetation.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:27.160
Now, make no mistake, some of these applications
were possible before, many of which we showcased

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:32.560
in previous videos, some of which you can
see here, what is new and interesting here

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:37.500
is that we have just one architecture here
that can handle many of these tasks.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.330
Beyond that, this architecture requires much
less data than previous techniques as it often

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:49.070
needs just one or at most a few images of
the target subject to do all this magic.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:54.519
The paper is ample in comparisons to these
other methods, for instance, the FID measures

00:02:54.519 --> 00:03:00.190
the quality and the diversity of the generated
output images, and is subject to minimization,

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:04.060
and you see that it is miles beyond these
previous works.

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:09.080
Some limitations also apply, if the inputs
stray too far away from topics that the neural

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:14.299
networks were trained on, we shouldn’t expect
results of this quality, and we are also dependent

00:03:14.299 --> 00:03:18.990
on proper inputs for the poses and segmentation
maps for it to work well.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:23.989
The pace of progress in machine learning research
is absolutely incredible, and we are getting

00:03:23.989 --> 00:03:29.489
very close to producing tools that can be
actively used to empower artists working in

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:30.800
the industry.

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:55.620
What a time to

00:03:55.620 --> 00:04:18.000
be alive!

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.290
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

